A pool's own node hangs from nothing rather than below the Untyped it was retyped from,
so the Untyped does not know it made the pool and is free at once:
it makes a frame over the pool, and a revoke below it does not destroy the pool.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work test qemu-replay
Report: memory that may hold kernel objects overlaps a node not above or below it

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -221,7 +221,7 @@ static int op_untyped(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
              * so the capability may go and the memory stays accounted for.
              * It is not zeroed here, which would be work in its size: pool_alloc zeroes each object.
              */
-            struct pool *pool = pool_create(base, size, u);
+            struct pool *pool = pool_create(base, size, NULL);
             c = cap_to_object(&pool->hdr, RIGHT_ALL);
             parent = &pool->node;
         } else {
